{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-123595",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250054/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${45}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
